        {
          "id": "pub-el-gaz-0025",
          "left": "36px",
          "top": "598px",
          "width": "228px",
          "height": "474px",
          "transform": "none",
          "zIndex": "10",
          "overflow": "visible",
          "type": "text",
          "innerHTML": "<div contenteditable=\"true\" spellcheck=\"false\" style=\"width:100%; height:100%; padding:0px; box-sizing:border-box; font-family:'Merriweather', Georgia, serif; font-size:9.8px; color:#1f2937; line-height:1.48; text-align:justify; outline:none;\"><div style=\"margin:0 0 11px 0;\"><span style=\"float:left; font-family:'Playfair Display', Georgia, serif; font-size:38px; line-height:0.84; font-weight:700; color:#0f5c57; padding:3px 7px 0 0;\">F</span>or four decades, neighborhood newspapers, parish bulletins, and school yearbooks shared the same quiet backbone: desktop publishing tools installed directly on local computers, unbothered by expired cloud tokens or annual price hikes.</div><div style=\"margin:0 0 11px 0;\">With legacy favorites such as Microsoft Publisher heading toward retirement in late 2026, small volunteer teams faced a frustrating choice between complex web paywalls and fragile word-processor workarounds.</div><div style=\"margin:0;\">OpenPublisher stepped straight into that gap with an unapologetically print-first ethos: download once, install on practically any machine you already own, and keep full custody of every booklet, menu, and broadsheet forever.</div></div>",
          "imgSrc": "",
          "clipPath": "",
          "bg": "",
          "cropMode": false,
          "imgStyle": {},
          "scaleX": "1",
          "scaleY": "1",
          "shrinkOverflow": false,
          "growFit": false,
          "arrowStyle": null,
          "arrowHead": null,
          "arrowStrokeWidth": null,
          "textboxStyle": null,
          "chartConfig": null,
          "isBorder": false,
          "isLocked": false
        },
        {
          "id": "pub-el-gaz-0026",
          "left": "288px",
          "top": "598px",
          "width": "228px",
          "height": "474px",
          "transform": "none",
          "zIndex": "10",
          "overflow": "visible",
          "type": "text",
          "innerHTML": "<div contenteditable=\"true\" spellcheck=\"false\" style=\"width:100%; height:100%; padding:0px; box-sizing:border-box; font-family:'Merriweather', Georgia, serif; font-size:9.8px; color:#1f2937; line-height:1.48; text-align:justify; outline:none;\"><div style=\"margin:0 0 11px 0;\">Unlike browser-only design SaaS portals that withhold export resolution behind recurring subscriptions, OpenPublisher embeds every font scheme, image asset, Master Page, and vector chart inside a self-contained <b>.opub</b> project file on your hard drive.</div><div style=\"font-family:'Playfair Display', Georgia, serif; font-size:12.5px; font-weight:700; color:#111827; margin:4px 0 6px 0;\">Designed for Real Editors</div><div style=\"margin:0 0 11px 0;\">Whether a school secretary needs a crisp booklet printed by 8:00 AM or a civic treasurer needs updated financial bar charts before tonight's AGM, every frame on this broadsheet stays editable with a single click.</div><div style=\"margin:0;\">From automatic saddle-stitch Booklet imposition to 3-zone Master Headers, desktop publishing is back where it belongs: under the editor's own roof.</div></div>",
          "imgSrc": "",
          "clipPath": "",
          "bg": "",
          "cropMode": false,
